That's kind of like how I've always felt like programming is like you kind of have this idea of where you want to go or what you want this thing to be and you slowly kind of put all the pieces together and and then at the end you have something so you get like that that you know that rush of creativity and creation kind of all packaged together in programming i feel like which is why i think so many people are drawn to it so speaking of having an idea and then slowly piecing it together and having something at the end uh let's talk about your app spend stack so this came out uh last year in july right i think that's right yeah it was shortly after wwdc after all my friends finally gave me enough trash that i I've been getting enough trash that I didn't release it.

202
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:26,720
So, you know, I had to.

203
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:30,960
So, I'll let you kind of give the pitch, because I know I'm not going to do it justice.

204
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:32,000
Yeah, no problem.

205
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:38,880
So, it's funny you say that because how I've seen media outlets cover it has kind of made the pitch better than I thought.

206
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:39,120
Yeah.

207
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:41,840
So, I've kind of been dealing what they say about it.

208
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,440
It turns out those guys are very good at writing and communicating ideas.

209
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:45,680
Yeah.

210
00:14:45,840 --> 00:14:46,720
They know what they're doing, right?

211
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:46,960
Yeah.

212
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:48,400
It's almost like they get paid for it.

213
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:48,800
Yeah.

214
00:14:49,680 --> 00:14:50,400
But anyways, yeah.

215
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:56,720
So the elevator pitch is like a spin stack is a way to make a list where you want to associate monetary values to the items.

216
00:14:57,360 --> 00:14:59,520
And it turns out there's a lot of use cases for that.

217
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:06,720
For me, the backstory slash origin story is my wife and I, we budget almost all in cash.

218
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:12,240
And when I would go to the grocery store, it was really hard to stay within the budget that we had.

219
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:14,960
And there's so many reasons for why that is.

220
00:15:15,440 --> 00:15:21,440
So if you're not in the United States, you may not know that sales tax isn't applied until you go and pay for the stuff.

221
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:23,440
So there's that aspect of it.

222
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:27,760
So I was like, okay, I'll build an app that tracks the sales tax and puts it in there.

223
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:32,400
And then there's the others, all these other little nuances, like there could be a discount.

224
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:34,880
It could be a fractional discount or a dollar amount.

225
00:15:35,360 --> 00:15:36,320
You could price things by weight.

226
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:38,400
or you can have multiple quantities of them.

227
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:40,640
And so I was like, this is the app that I want to make.

228
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:41,840
I want to solve that problem.

229
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:46,480
And so for me, one of the biggest use cases has always been like grocery shopping.

230
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:51,760
And it's funny because almost no one that I see that bought the app uses it for that.

231
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:57,120
They use it for like general lists, yeah, right, that they want to just keep track of.

232
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:06,400
So like home remodels or budgeting for vacation, planning birthday parties, like anything where you're like, okay, I need to buy some stuff and I want to know how much it's going to cost.

233
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:10,880
Yeah, I saw your wife wrote a post about using it for planning Christmas gifts.

234
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:13,760
And that was when it clicked for me as a something that I could use.

235
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:14,640
Yeah, totally.

236
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:16,000
And that was a perfect use case.

237
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:23,200
So, you know, we shared the list together and we got all the live edits and stuff and we just kind of planned all our entire Christmas that way.

238
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,760
So life has all these little situations where you just want to know how much stuff it's going to cost.

239
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:30,080
And that's really what I built it for.

240
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:33,920
And then what it's being used for kind of spans the map.

241
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:38,080
Some people use it for full on budgeting and want to like connect up their bank account.

242
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:40,960
You know, they go really like far into that aspect of it.

243
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:43,440
And some people are just a little bit more casual like I am.

244
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:45,360
Like, oh, there's just a few things that I need to add up.

245
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:48,320
You know, I use it like a few times a month and that works for me.

246
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,920
So that's been its greatest strength and weakness in a way.

247
00:16:52,080 --> 00:17:00,400
It's kind of like a jack of all trades, master of none kind of thing, which has made it a little bit difficult to like lean into a product direction because everyone uses it differently.

248
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,960
But yeah, the elevator pitch is, I got some stuff, I want to know how much it costs.

249
00:17:05,280 --> 00:17:06,960
So that's what SpinStack would do for you.

250
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:13,680
So like I kind of joked about earlier and you joked about, this was an extremely long process to build this app, right?

251
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:13,920
Yeah.

252
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,280
So like, oh yeah.

253
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:18,240
Can you kind of talk through like why it took so long?

254
00:17:18,640 --> 00:17:20,240
What, did it make sense that it took so long?

255
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,560
Like, would you do it the same again or was there some kind of bumps along the way or big breaks or something?

256
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:26,800
Yeah, you know, this is a really good question.

257
00:17:27,360 --> 00:17:29,360
And I get asked this quite a bit.

258
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:36,080
And at my own expense, because I always joke about it too, because I just think it's funny.

259
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:41,600
But to preface it, like, it all comes back to like what your goals are with the project.

260
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,440
And for me, the goal was I did want to make money on it, right?

261
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:50,320
Because for me, I don't, I'm not motivated to keep working on something if no one's going to like buy it.

262
00:17:50,640 --> 00:17:52,720
I found that out really early in my career.

263
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:56,000
So I knew I did want to make money, but it wasn't like the main factor.

264
00:17:56,960 --> 00:18:12,480
My main factor was like to solve my own problem, but even more than that., I wanted to make an app that I was really, really, really proud of, that just felt well built and well designed and was like a good model of like what a good iOS app should look and feel like.

265
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:20,400
So I placed that over pretty much anything else, and so that is the short story of like why it took so long.

266
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:25,440
But the longer story is I have this post called Can you learn design?

267
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:35,680
And if you look at it, you'll see that the first version of SpinStack that I released in like, I don't know, 2012 or 2013 or something like that, the design was real bad.

268
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:47,680
Like, you know, objectively bad, like really bright colors that clash, the contrast was weird, there were these like hidden gestures, like, you know, I would find a new project on GitHub that I thought was cool and just kinda throw it in there just because it was neat, you know.

269
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:54,480
But it was a really good lesson because I learned like, no one's going to really use this if the design isn't good.

270
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:56,560
So you did, you released that version?

271
00:18:56,800 --> 00:18:58,960
Oh yeah, yeah, it was out in the App Store for sure.

272
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:04,400
And I still, I've been wanting to do like a conference talk on it one time because I still have it in GitHub so I can boot it up.

273
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:10,320
And, you know, I've got this weird like, kind of walkthrough that has like these cartoon vector graphics of like a house in a sky.

274
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:11,600
I have no idea why.

275
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:16,960
And it kind of panes, what's the word I'm looking for?

276
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:18,800
I can't think of it.

277
00:19:18,880 --> 00:19:24,000
But it kind of panes with two backgrounds like on top of each other as it, like, Oh, parallax.

278
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:24,640
Yes, thank you.

279
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:25,520
Yes, parallax.

280
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:32,480
But, but, yeah, so I just spent so much time trying to, like, dig up apps that were really well done.

281
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:39,440
And along that journey, I just kind of resigned to the thought that, like, Apple really knows what they're doing with apps, obviously, right?

282
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:45,600
It sounds like kind of a weird statement, but there are people who vehemently disagree with it since the iOS 7 redesign.

283
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:48,000
We won't, we won't open up that can of worms.

284
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:48,880
Exactly.

285
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:49,360
Yeah.

286
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:55,280
So I'll just say my piece on it is that Apple's the one that's like handing out the awards and like making choices.

287
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:57,280
So I'm going to listen to what they're saying.

288
00:19:57,840 --> 00:20:11,680
And so I read the human interface guideline from top to bottom, like literally from the first page to the end, and I do that every year now, and I just looked at apps that were doing it really well and just spent so much time learning about design.

289
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:16,720
So I would say most of that was designing it and making it feel right.

290
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:20,880
And yeah, I mean it ended up looking a lot better than it did before.

291
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:23,680
But that's that's where all the time came from.

292
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:33,840
And of course, as the years go by, you become a better programmer and you want to clean things up and refactor it just because it feels like you're making progress even though it's not really doing anything.

293
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:37,440
You just feel, Oh, I'm making some progress because I rewrote the same thing 15 times.

294
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:38,480
Exactly.

295
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:40,480
Well, so I kind of got into that too.

296
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:50,400
And yeah, that was what I was going to ask is like, I think especially people listening to the show probably can really relate to the idea of having a project that you start.

297
00:20:50,880 --> 00:21:00,720
And then it kind of gets into this lull where you're working on it constantly, but you're not really necessarily, like, it doesn't feel like the end date is ever getting closer.

298
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:07,040
And eventually there's a point where you realize, I'm just going to keep working on this, but it's never actually going to get across the finish line.

299
00:21:07,440 --> 00:21:22,320
So, like, where did things change for you where it went from this thing that was just sort of this, you know, your backyard playground that you've been building for ten years or something, into a thing that you were actually going to release into the world?

300
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:31,760
Was there a point where you suddenly decided, I'm going to finish this up and I'm going to do the last minute polish and actually put together press releases and all that stuff?

301
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:36,720
Or were you really on a multiyear plan and you just kind of finished that?

302
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:37,200
out.

303
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:57,200
Yeah, so the short answer to that is I after WWEC, yeah, what would have been last year, 2019, I was talking to, so my colleague Andy is he's always been giving me a hard time about like, you know, not shipping it because if you follow him on Twitter, he ships like a side project like every weekend.

304
00:21:57,600 --> 00:21:58,160
It's crazy.

305
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:00,400
He's got a million side projects.

306
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,880
But he's like, you know, you need to ship it and figure out, you know, what's working, what's not working.

307
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:06,640
And I'm like, yeah, you know, whatever, I might.

308
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:08,720
I just really want to make like a great app.

309
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:17,280
And then when I was having lunch with my friend Dave, who runs iOS Dev Weekly.

310
00:22:18,320 --> 00:22:19,360
We were kind of joking about it.

311
00:22:19,520 --> 00:22:22,320
And I kept showing him these new enhancements and changes that I made.

312
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:27,360
And I had a lab at WWDC, a design lab with some of the Apple designers.

313
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:28,800
And they're like, oh yeah, this looks really good.

314
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:30,640
And I got encouraging feedback on it.

315
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:33,920
But Dave was just kept saying, like, yeah, but have you shipped it yet?

316
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:36,080
I was like, no, but it looks cool, doesn't it?

317
00:22:36,240 --> 00:22:40,320
And he's like, yeah, but it doesn't matter if no one besides you or your wife ever sees it.

318
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:43,120
And I was like, yeah, that's a great point.

319
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:46,240
So what I did is I finally, after WWDC, I got home.

320
00:22:46,880 --> 00:22:57,280
And the turning point was like, I was wanted to get everything with iOS 13 in, but I knew that I would just keep doing that annually over and over after every WWDC.

321
00:22:57,440 --> 00:23:02,560
So that's where I put the proverbial foot down and said, Okay, I'm going to ship this like in a month.

322
00:23:02,880 --> 00:23:04,080
And that's what I ended up doing.

323
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:09,200
I just, it's funny because I just kind of shipped it one day, you know, like without much thought.

324
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:11,120
I was just like, Okay, I just need to get it out.

325
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:12,320
I'm just hitting the button.

326
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:13,440
Just hitting the button.

327
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:14,480
I made the classic mistake.

328
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:18,240
I told the press like a week before that it was going out, you know.

329
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:23,680
Yeah, that was one question I had is like, what kind of marketing and everything did you do?

330
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:30,560
It sounds like you gave yourself a month, but it sounds like the app was already in a pretty good state at that point as far as development goes.

331
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:30,880
Yeah.

332
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:32,800
Oh, no, it was in a really good state.

333
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:39,280
So at one of our work retreats, our CEO at Buffer, Joel, is he loves talking about side projects.

334
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:47,120
So, you know, I had a little sit down with him and one thing that he said that resonated was like, Jordan, the version of SpinStack that you have is like version three.

335
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:49,760
Like it's full featured, like it's polished.

336
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:56,000
You know, and this was a recurring theme, anyone I talked to about it said, like you just need to ship it at this point.

337
00:23:56,240 --> 00:23:57,360
Like you've worked on it for years.

338
00:23:57,920 --> 00:23:59,600
It's time to let it go into the world.

339
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:01,760
And, you know, flutter its wings.

340
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:08,240
So the marketing is like probably the thing I messed up the most, mostly around timing, right?

341
00:24:08,640 --> 00:24:20,640
Because you want to give the press, I would say, like a month, at least a month heads up so they can get the beta build, they can form some thoughts and opinions on it, you know, they can use it, you can have a beta period, let people try it out.

342
00:24:21,520 --> 00:24:29,440
And actually, I think this is something you did really, really well with Dark Noise, something I definitely took notes on kind of like your timeline, because, you know, you talked about it for a while.

343
00:24:29,920 --> 00:24:35,680
I'm sure, you know, the press had it, and that gives everyone like a nice introduction to what you're building.

344
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:48,800
Well, I have to say, your post whenever you released, which was when I was kind of in the throes of working on Dark Noise, your post ended with the thing I messed up on is I didn't give the press enough time before releasing it.

345
00:24:48,960 --> 00:24:52,240
And I was like, oh, wait a minute, I'm planning to release this soon.

346
00:24:52,480 --> 00:24:57,600
And that was that literally that exact sentence was when I started figuring out press stuff.

347
00:24:57,920 --> 00:25:02,240
So your post talking about this is the reason that I did all that.

348
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:03,280
That's so good.

349
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:07,360
Let my mistakes in 2021 or the past save you some headache.

350
00:25:07,520 --> 00:25:08,880
So yeah, you know, it's funny.

351
00:25:09,040 --> 00:25:13,440
And because, you know, I have so many posts about like what a best in class app is and stuff.

352
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:18,080
And internally, I didn't want to release FinStack until I had everything on that list done.

353
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:23,440
And so if you've seen the list, it would have taken me like at least another year to do that.

354
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:27,040
And that's like the state in my mind that I wanted to ship it in.

355
00:25:27,440 --> 00:25:30,800
I was going to be like, this is like, you know, my peak right here.

356
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:31,920
This is my crown jewel.

357
00:25:32,080 --> 00:25:32,480
It's perfect.

358
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:33,840
It's just how I imagined it.

359
00:25:34,560 --> 00:25:38,160
But even for someone like me who's in that, I realized like, that's not going to work.

360
00:25:38,320 --> 00:25:42,000
Like, I have to get it out and then work towards that best in class checklist.

361
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:42,400
Yeah.

362
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:48,560
And like, if you guys don't know what he's talking about, he has a post on his blog that I'll link to in the show notes that.

363
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:57,600
that it literally is a giant checklist broken up into different sections of what this is just what you consider to be a best in class app, right?

364
00:25:57,920 --> 00:25:58,080
Right.

365
00:25:58,240 --> 00:25:58,320
Yeah.

366
00:25:58,720 --> 00:26:01,600
And this is based on like, and you sort of lay it out in the post.

367
00:26:01,840 --> 00:26:08,320
It's based on what you considered your North Star apps, like the apps that you aspire to sort of be slash Apple apps themselves.

368
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:15,520
And it goes from like accessibility to design to a bunch of other little vectors, but it's it's very big.

369
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:17,280
Oh, it's huge.

370
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:17,520
Yeah.

371
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:20,480
And it, and really no app can like ship in that state.

372
00:26:20,640 --> 00:26:22,880
You know, there's so many things like, like little things.

373
00:26:23,040 --> 00:26:27,600
Like, you know, we're kind of in that bubble of knowing what the press likes.

374
00:26:28,080 --> 00:26:32,720
And what I mean by that is like, I didn't want to send it to Max Stories until I had serious shortcuts, right?

375
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:33,440
Because like, yeah.

376
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:36,080
I know that Federico loves shortcuts.

377
00:26:36,160 --> 00:26:36,960
Like, that's his thing.

378
00:26:37,920 --> 00:26:39,280
And he loves the iPad app.

379
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:47,920
So I, you know, I wanted to have full keyboard navigation in there, but even for someone like, I'm a huge supporter of like, taking all the time you want on your Indie apps.

380
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:51,760
For me, I even got to the point where I was like, okay, this is, this is getting ridiculous.

381
00:26:51,920 --> 00:26:53,280
Like, I have to ignore.

382
00:26:54,960 --> 00:27:04,560
But I will say one thing that went really well marketing wise was, and I've said this over and over to anyone that will listen., it's like just kind of telling your story about why you made the app that you made.

383
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:13,520
And it's funny because I just kind of threw a post together and it took like, I don't know, I spent like a day on it, but it was just, it was just real talk.

384
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:16,320
Like, hey, it's kind of funny between me and my wife.

385
00:27:16,480 --> 00:27:26,560
We've been working on SpinStack through like each child delivery and I realized that I still had a picture of me in the hospital through each of my kids being born of me working on SpinStack.

386
00:27:26,720 --> 00:27:30,560
So I was like, hey, that's kind of like a fun human angle to throw at it.

387
00:27:31,360 --> 00:27:35,040
But I also made sure to answer like, what is SpinStack and like, why would someone want to use it?

388
00:27:35,840 --> 00:27:39,120
But, but wow., that really did well for me.

389
00:27:39,520 --> 00:27:42,640
And it's one of those things, like you never know what's going to resonate with people.

390
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:57,280
And I think when you kind of are just real in a way, and I know that's like such a stereotypical thing to say, but if you just kind of humanize, that's a better way to say, if you just kind of humanize who you are and what you're doing and why you built what you built, it makes it much more relatable to people.

391
00:27:58,240 --> 00:28:03,200
And that, I think, introduced a lot of people to Spin Stack and definitely helped me out a ton.

392
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:09,600
So that's one piece of advice I always tell everybody, it's like really kind of humanize the story behind why you made what you made.

393
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:19,440
And within that, you can sneak in your L elevator pitch for the app and, you know, people don't even really realize they're being sold to at that point because it's not really a sales pitch.

394
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:20,960
It's kind of an origin story, right?

395
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:22,480
And people love origin stories.

396
00:28:22,720 --> 00:28:22,800
Yeah.

397
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:40,240
It's why, like, you know, movies with, like, well known directors or, or companies like, like Tesla or something, where, you know, it's a it's a little bit disingenuous, but we all think of them as being run or built by this kind of singular person and singular vision.

398
00:28:40,320 --> 00:28:43,760
And that person usually is very good at telling their sort of story.

399
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:52,160
And we all know the story of, you know, Tesla and how Elon Musk figured all this stuff out or had these visions or whatever.

400
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:59,920
And in those cases, we know in the back of our heads that there's this giant machine of a business behind it and all these other people.

401
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:04,960
But the reason we sort of latch on to those is because of this sort of human element of this one person.

402
00:29:05,360 --> 00:29:09,280
And one of the things that indies have an advantage of is it's not disingenuous.

403
00:29:09,520 --> 00:29:14,000
It's completely accurate to say that this is a very personal story and it's this one person.

404
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:15,600
And you can just say it.

405
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:19,760
You don't need a marketing team because it's just kind of the truth.

406
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:25,520
And probably or oftentimes, a lot of people will completely resonate with that because they're also people.

407
00:29:25,920 --> 00:29:48,720
And especially in our case with the iOS community that's very tight knit, especially on Twitter, those people at the very least will resonate with it because a lot of times, at least in your case, I didn't know who you were before SpinStack came out and I started devouring everything that you made after that because it was like, this guy is going through the same thing as I am, granted, a lot better and over a longer period of time.

408
00:29:48,960 --> 00:29:54,960
But that type of thing, like, made me extremely interested in your story and your product.

409
00:29:55,600 --> 00:30:00,240
And I think that probably relates to a lot of other people in the same vein.

410
00:30:00,400 --> 00:30:00,480
Yeah.

411
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:07,280
That's like one of the best things about the IOS community is I feel like everyone really wants to share a lot and they're eager to share.

412
00:30:07,520 --> 00:30:14,160
And I think that's really, I think that's a good thing because it kind of levels the playing field and makes other people feel like, oh, this is, you know, this is something I can do too.

413
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:23,440
And then you kind of learn little bites and pieces from everyone else's experience, you know, like in my case, you kind of like read the part where it's like, oh, I should reach out to the press.

414
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:31,600
And in your case, you know, I jotted down like notes from your launch too that that made it successful and like, oh yeah, that's something I can use for my next product.

415
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:43,760
So I feel like it just kind of is like a snowball effect of all of us just kind of putting out these things that worked and then it kind of hopefully will mass up into some giant success story, I think, for someone one day.

416
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:50,080
And it's funny because there's also that stigma that, you know, you just can't do anything in the app store.

417
00:30:50,240 --> 00:30:51,120
It's too saturated.

418
00:30:51,680 --> 00:30:52,000
It's done.

419
00:30:52,240 --> 00:31:01,440
But I feel like there's a little bit of a renaissance, if I may say so myself, from indie developers putting out stuff that's kind of sticking and working and having some success.

420
00:31:03,040 --> 00:31:10,720
And again, whatever success looks like to you, I'm definitely not quitting my day job at Buffer nor would I want to anyway from Spinstacks Income.

421
00:31:12,160 --> 00:31:15,760
But, you know, it's to me, it's been successful and it's been so fun to make.

422
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:23,840
And I feel like there's a lot of encouraging results like that happening within the IOS developer community lately.

423
00:31:24,080 --> 00:31:28,080
Yeah, I think we're over trying to repeat the gold rush era.

424
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:29,440
Yeah, absolutely.

425
00:31:29,600 --> 00:31:34,160
And I think the bottom line is like, there's just room for great apps and there always will be.

426
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:42,880
Because sooner or later, even if there's established players, like, I mean, the white noise market has been around since the advent of the app store, right?

427
00:31:43,040 --> 00:31:44,160
Like the very beginning.

428
00:31:44,240 --> 00:31:44,320
Yeah.

429
00:31:44,560 --> 00:31:45,040
Yeah, exactly.

430
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:46,960
So dark noise is a perfect example of that.

431
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:50,800
Like, there's room for someone to come in and make something that's just really well built.

432
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:54,800
And I think for me, that's the thing I'm most passionate about when it comes to iOS development.

433
00:31:54,960 --> 00:32:00,320
Like, I really just want to build something that's really good and would make like Apple proud.

434
00:32:00,560 --> 00:32:08,000
And it's kind of silly to say it out loud, but like, you know, some people they want to make money and build like a great product and do product market fit.

435
00:32:08,800 --> 00:32:09,360
And that's great.

436
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
But it's just like not really what I'm doing.

437
00:32:12,960 --> 00:32:21,360
Because if I were, I'd be hopping on the mountain of feature requests I have and moving towards those, which to be fair, I am doing a lot of those right now to make it a better product.

438
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,480
But above all else, like, I just want it to be an example.

439
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:31,520
of if someone's like, what's an app that's just done really good and for them to think of spin stack.

440
00:32:31,680 --> 00:32:34,560
And then, and like you said, I have those North Star apps where I think of that now.

441
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:37,280
Like Things three is definitely one of them.

442
00:32:38,240 --> 00:32:39,360
You know, IA Rider.

443
00:32:39,680 --> 00:32:44,400
Yeah, things, playing around with your app, it, it makes me think of things a lot.

444
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:50,480
I mean, given it's a list app as well, but it's that, like, almost obnoxious level of polish.

445
00:32:51,040 --> 00:32:59,520
Like, every single thing that you do, like, this is probably not a normal person, but as a developer, it almost makes me angry because you're like, ah, he did that?

446
00:33:00,480 --> 00:33:02,560
Goodness, me, like I don't have time for that, you know.

447
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:03,360
I know what you mean.

448
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:04,400
I know exactly what you mean.

449
00:33:04,560 --> 00:33:05,760
And that's what Things is like.

450
00:33:05,920 --> 00:33:08,160
But in my head, Things I don't associate with a person.

451
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:09,360
I think of them as a big team.

452
00:33:09,920 --> 00:33:15,120
And so every time I open your app to use as a sort of example or like, how did he solve this design problem?

453
00:33:15,520 --> 00:33:18,240
I'm always like, almost frustrated because I'm like, I can't.

454
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:19,200
I just don't have time for that.

455
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:20,080
Like, good night.

456
00:33:20,960 --> 00:33:22,880
Well, don't feel bad because I took like five years.

457
00:33:25,200 --> 00:33:36,240
But it really is, it's just like, part of the reason that I was so excited about doing iOS stuff and getting to do a side project in this space was, unlike a real jobby job.

458
00:33:36,240 --> 00:33:43,920
But when you're doing a TV job, you can make choices that aren't necessarily the most business sense, and you're sort of allowed to care about all the things that you care about.

459
00:33:44,240 --> 00:33:44,880
Oh, absolutely.

460
00:33:45,120 --> 00:33:48,240
Even if they don't really matter to the customer or the user or whatever.

461
00:33:48,560 --> 00:33:56,000
And that's weird to say, and if you're building a business, you should have that mindset, although allow yourself to have some indulgences every now and then.

462
00:33:56,240 --> 00:34:02,400
But when it's a total side project, you just get to indulge yourself for all the little nice things that you have.

463
00:34:02,800 --> 00:34:08,240
You can make an animated icon for every single sound in your white noise app, even though I love that, by the way.

464
00:34:08,320 --> 00:34:09,680
That's a great touch.

465
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:12,000
I thought that was going to be like, people would just think it's dumb.

466
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:15,360
And when I mentioned it to a lot of people, they're just like, that's going to take you forever.

467
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:19,600
I'm like, yeah, but that's kind of the point is it's an excuse for me to get to do this stuff, right?

468
00:34:20,080 --> 00:34:20,560
Absolutely.

469
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:27,200
But whenever people like you do that, it gets to like get people excited.

470
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:31,280
You know, whenever you open it, it's really delightful to use and did that have the ROI?

471
00:34:31,520 --> 00:34:36,000
Was it really worth it in terms of time spent and money that you get out of sales?

472
00:34:36,480 --> 00:34:40,880
Maybe not, but it's something that's really fun for people to look at.

473
00:34:41,360 --> 00:34:47,680
And what I think is really cool, and I can definitely say this about your app, is it affects the ecosystem as a whole.

474
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:53,920
Like your app, the work that you did not only made your users happy, but like affected decisions that I made.

475
00:34:54,560 --> 00:35:02,080
And it's cool to think that maybe there are people that are playing around with my app and using that as an example for what they do.

476
00:35:02,320 --> 00:35:08,880
And like you have this actual, you know, little dent in the universe in Apple parlance for the work that you do.

477
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:11,520
And that's just like super exciting.

478
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:13,200
Oh, it's a blast.

479
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:13,440
Yeah.

480
00:35:13,760 --> 00:36:06,480
And you're totally right about that because it's funny you mentioned that point because I just tweeted something similar yesterday morning like, hey, you know, like I don't know if maybe one of these things I took a lot of time on is like the thing that pushed it over the edge to you know get noticed by apple or by users and the point you made about like the animated icons when you choose a noise and dark noise i i read about that i think in every review that i read of dark noise because you know like i was doing i was like oh cool who car uh he got a ton of coverage like maybe these are some people i could reach out to so you know i was reading through some of the reviews and all of them mentioned that so it's like you never know like what that small little touch is that's going to push it over you know the hump so to speak of being covered or not being covered by people um and it's just and it's hard when you're in it because you can just have analysis paralysis and just keep going over and over all these things that you might need to do or not need to do.

481
00:36:07,440 --> 00:36:11,680
And again, in like Apple parlance, like saying no to things is just as important as saying yes.

482
00:36:11,840 --> 00:36:16,800
But yeah, indulgence is like a great word for spin stacks development because that's all I did.

483
00:36:17,120 --> 00:36:18,480
I like over indulged in everything.

484
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:21,120
You know, like what, what's a nice little touch?

485
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:28,320
Like even one of my favorites was my mom, you know, she's a little getting to that point where she needs to use glasses for quite a few things.

486
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:35,520
And when there's accessibility sizes on in spin stack, like I made the table view cell separators like thicker too.

487
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:37,760
Because she mentioned like they're a little too small.

488
00:36:38,240 --> 00:36:41,120
And so it's like little things like that that you take the extra time on.

489
00:36:41,360 --> 00:36:47,360
Like you just, who's to say what's going to make it more of a success for you if you hadn't done those things.

490
00:36:47,840 --> 00:36:54,960
And that's what's fun about side projects, because you get to, you know, burn that time on it if you want to just make a great product more so than, you know, run a business.

491
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:59,760
So you you've mentioned like different people playing around with it beforehand.

492
00:37:00,240 --> 00:37:04,160
Did you run a, a sort of beta group of some sort?

493
00:37:04,400 --> 00:37:12,480
Did you just share it with friends to kind of back up when it first released that first version, though, and this is another thing I need to put in a blog post like the local press.

494
00:37:13,600 --> 00:37:15,760
loved SpinStack, even though it was a bad app.

495
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:19,200
Like they loved it because back in that back in that day, right?

496
00:37:19,360 --> 00:37:24,160
I feel old saying that, but back in those times, like making an app was still a novelty.

497
00:37:24,480 --> 00:37:27,600
And so, and the community college loved it too, that I had released like an app.

498
00:37:27,760 --> 00:37:31,520
Like, hey, here's one of our students that learned iOS development, you know, that kind of angle.

499
00:37:31,760 --> 00:37:37,040
So, like, I went on local TV stations and stuff and like, showed them the app and they wanted to do this whole piece over it.

500
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:41,040
So, people locally knew about it just from that.

501
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:59,280
And so when I, you know, quickly abandoned that app and went back to the cutting board, my mom and Jansen, my daug wife, had always used it throughout the years, which was great feedback because if you haven't had your mom use your app, like you absolutely should, and you can put in air quotes whoever your mom is.

502
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:04,960
Like my mom is not tech savvy, like she has an iPhone and she knows how to use it half the time, you know, right?

503
00:38:06,320 --> 00:38:11,200
But you give those people your app and you're going to cover a lot of edge cases and things that don't make sense that you thought made sense.

504
00:38:11,360 --> 00:38:12,960
Yeah, that was me with my kids.

505
00:38:13,440 --> 00:38:14,800
My son was Oh, absolutely.

506
00:38:15,200 --> 00:38:19,840
three years old whenever, no, four years old whenever, whenever it came out.

507
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:23,040
And I, like, was letting him play with it all the time.

508
00:38:23,200 --> 00:38:29,440
And they just got to where they thought of it as a game, but it was fascinating watching them navigate the UI and figure it out.

509
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:34,320
Obviously that probably wouldn't be the case with a money tracking app, but.

510
00:38:34,960 --> 00:38:36,240
Yes, now put your allowance in.

511
00:38:36,320 --> 00:38:50,400
Yeah, little Charlie, it's time to track that stuff, but other than that, my colleague Andy was the only other one that had used it, and yeah, so that's another thing I would have done differently is had like an open beta period, but yeah, very, very few people used it.

512
00:38:50,480 --> 00:39:00,880
So there were a lot of blind spots in the app that I didn't figure out until I shipped it, and one glaring one was when I did the, you know, side project Saturday on the Apple subreddit.

513
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:03,520
Some people are like, hey, is it going to release in my territory?

514
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:08,400
And at first I just did the United States and Canada because I knew like the sales tax thing was really important.

515
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:09,040
Oh, right.

516
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:09,200
Yeah.

517
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:11,040
And I was like, okay, sure, you know, whatever.

518
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:14,480
So I went in App Store Connect and popped in every territory.

519
00:39:15,040 --> 00:39:25,600
And when it shipped, I got like hit with several one-star reviews because there was basically a logic where I formatted currency that didn't work for non-US territories.

520
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:30,400
So like people in, you know, Europe couldn't really enter in anything.

521
00:39:30,640 --> 00:39:34,000
And it's just like if I had a beta, that would have been something I caught right away.

522
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:36,560
But instead, I had to like ship an update like in.

523
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:39,200
in six hours the next day when I see it.

524
00:39:39,280 --> 00:39:39,520
Oh man.

525
00:39:40,240 --> 00:39:40,320
Yeah.

526
00:39:40,560 --> 00:39:44,240
Everyone being like, Hey, this was great, but I can't, also can't use it.

527
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:52,000
And, you know, luckily being in the iOS development community, they're really nice if they bought your app and, you know, people contact out to you on Twitter and let you know.

528
00:39:52,240 --> 00:39:58,400
But the real, like, customers who don't know anything about you, like, they're pegging you with the one star review pretty quick.

529
00:39:58,960 --> 00:40:03,040
So, more of the story, indie developers out there, have a beta period.

530
00:40:03,440 --> 00:40:04,640
It's definitely a good thing to do.

531
00:40:05,200 --> 00:40:10,000
And so, like, you've mentioned that you were sort of surprised by how people were actually using it.

532
00:40:10,240 --> 00:40:12,320
They were sort of using it differently than what you were expecting.

533
00:40:13,600 --> 00:40:16,560
What are the different methods that you've used to try and collect that feedback?

534
00:40:17,520 --> 00:40:21,440
Preferably, I assume one star reviews are not the way you want to hear about these things.

535
00:40:22,640 --> 00:40:24,720
It is a way, not the best way, for sure.

536
00:40:26,800 --> 00:40:28,320
Yeah, no, that's a really good question.

537
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:34,240
So, one, I just I just started asking people on support emails or feature request emails.

538
00:40:35,120 --> 00:40:37,040
I would just say, hey, you know, thanks for using the app.

539
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:38,880
I'd answer whatever, you know, they asked about.

540
00:40:39,040 --> 00:40:43,840
And then I would just say, if you don't mind, like, can you just reply back with one or two sentences like, how do you use the app?

541
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:49,200
And then someone on the Spinstack subreddit actually asked the same question, like, what are your use cas cases?

542
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:50,240
Like, what do you use it for?

543
00:40:51,200 --> 00:40:53,360
And then on Twitter DMs, I always ask that too.

544
00:40:53,760 --> 00:40:55,040
If someone reaches out about it.

545
00:40:55,600 --> 00:41:02,880
And through, you know, kind of all this feedback I've gathered, it's very clear that it's on a wide spectrum of use cases.

546
00:41:03,440 --> 00:41:06,400
And so I kind of like, I said at the beginning of the show, I really like that.

547
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:09,120
It means it's flexible and can fit a lot of needs.

548
00:41:09,760 --> 00:41:15,440
But it definitely makes the product aspect difficult because, again, as I mentioned, I just don't know which way to lean into.

549
00:41:15,920 --> 00:41:18,720
Like, do I let people who want to connect bank accounts do that?

550
00:41:18,880 --> 00:41:21,840
But, you know, then that kind of changes the optics of what the app is.

551
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:22,320
Right.

552
00:41:23,040 --> 00:41:23,840
And a little scary.

553
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:24,480
Yeah, exactly.

554
00:41:24,880 --> 00:41:29,200
And then I keep going back and forth on like, is it a productivity app or is it a finance app?

555
00:41:29,440 --> 00:41:34,000
And I I feel like every update I talk myself one way or the other, so I like, change it.

556
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:37,040
And so it'll go to finance and it'll go back to productivity.

557
00:41:38,080 --> 00:41:46,560
And I always wonder too, if that was, like, to my detriment, because like, you know, Apple asked me for the promo artwork and, to my knowledge, it's never been used.

558
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:49,760
And like, you always wonder like, did that, like, kind of hurt my chances?

559
00:41:49,840 --> 00:41:53,120
Because they, like, wanted to use it in productivity, then I switched it to finance.

560
00:41:53,440 --> 00:41:56,480
Or then they're like, okay, I'll use it in finance, then I switched it back to productivity.

561
00:41:56,560 --> 00:41:59,600
So, like, you've changed the actual category in the app store?

562
00:41:59,680 --> 00:42:00,880
Yeah, like, two or three times..

563
00:42:01,360 --> 00:42:01,440
Okay.

564
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:01,840
Yeah.

565
00:42:02,160 --> 00:42:03,680
I've swapped spin stack all over the place.

566
00:42:04,080 --> 00:42:08,320
It's just, it's taking its app store tour on each sales category, I guess.

567
00:42:09,840 --> 00:42:13,120
But yeah, so there's, yeah, who's to say?

568
00:42:13,520 --> 00:42:14,000
You never know.

569
00:42:14,240 --> 00:42:24,080
And in one of your posts, you had talked about sort of making a mission statement for your app, which that was something that I didn't have exactly as a mission statement, but I did sort of similar things.

570
00:42:25,040 --> 00:42:29,760
Because for me, the whole thing was like I was playing house acting like I was a project manager or something.

571
00:42:30,080 --> 00:42:31,040
But that's awesome.

572
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:36,720
Though, like your post about that was really helpful to help sort of help me forward.

573
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:40,400
Help me form decisions in terms of how do you want to take things.

574
00:42:40,560 --> 00:42:49,520
But it almost it almost sounds like since then you're sort of struggling with that specific part because of seeing the actual people who have used it, using it in different ways.

575
00:42:49,840 --> 00:42:51,360
Yeah, it's like this existential question.

576
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:57,760
Like what happens when the way people use your app doesn't really align with the app's mission statement of why you built it?

577
00:42:58,720 --> 00:42:58,880
You know?

578
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:04,960
So it's it it opens up a whole can of worms and that's where people who are great at product really excel.

579
00:43:05,280 --> 00:43:14,640
And that's, you know, one good thing about working with other people is because they can dig into that, really do a lot of information gathering and figure out what's the best way to go.

580
00:43:15,440 --> 00:43:21,120
I think Curtis Herbert had a really good post about this, or he must have mentioned it on Independence, I think.

581
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:22,960
God rest its soul.

582
00:43:23,120 --> 00:43:23,600
I love that show.

583
00:43:23,840 --> 00:43:24,640
Sad to see it go.

584
00:43:24,880 --> 00:43:33,600
But basically he was saying something like he had hired someone to do some market research more or less for slopes, his app.

585
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:36,560
And he found out a lot of interesting things.

586
00:43:37,040 --> 00:43:42,480
