738
00:55:47,680 --> 00:55:50,960
So, a pro tip., don't launch your app right before a family vacation.

739
00:55:51,440 --> 00:55:52,560
I probably won't do that again.

740
00:55:53,360 --> 00:55:57,280
Yeah, yeah, that seems like that would be, you wouldn't be able to keep it out of your mind.

741
00:55:57,760 --> 00:55:58,640
Oh yeah, it was bad.

742
00:55:58,800 --> 00:56:04,960
Like, I, once everyone went to bed, then I just, you know, cracked my iPad and tried to get through all the emails and tried to get the feedback.

743
00:56:05,120 --> 00:56:10,800
And, you know, luckily now that it's been in, kind of been out in the app store a while, I only get like a few support emails a week.

744
00:56:10,880 --> 00:56:11,920
So it's like really manageable.

745
00:56:12,080 --> 00:56:16,080
But, but at that time, right when it launched, it was like, you know, twenty or thirty a day.

746
00:56:16,160 --> 00:56:19,600
So I was like, oh my goodness, this feels like I have like two jobs right now.

747
00:56:20,000 --> 00:56:24,480
Now, it's interesting, like you mentioned this in your post and it was kind of influential.

748
00:56:25,120 --> 00:56:26,640
in some choices that I made too.

749
00:56:27,200 --> 00:56:32,160
One of the reasons you decided to go straight paid up front was for that exact reason, right?

750
00:56:32,560 --> 00:56:52,880
You wanted to almost put an artificial barrier on entry to the app so that you as an indie dev aren't getting just completely overwhelmed with support emails, because if it was free with some sort of in-app purchase or whatever, then every single person that downloads the free one potentially is a person who's going to send a support question.

751
00:56:53,600 --> 00:56:54,560
That's absolutely right.

752
00:56:54,880 --> 00:56:54,960
Yeah.

753
00:56:55,360 --> 00:57:00,000
And that was my thinking there is like, so if you just do some back of the napkin math, like you can.

754
00:57:00,720 --> 00:57:04,080
pretty safely say you would 10x your downloads if you went free.

755
00:57:04,880 --> 00:57:11,920
So you do 200 times 10 and then the 30 emails I was getting a day times 10, that's potentially like 300 emails.

756
00:57:12,640 --> 00:57:13,840
There's no way I can manage that.

757
00:57:15,120 --> 00:57:25,680
And some people that works really well for them and they kind of optimize and plan for that and that's their model is they want to get a ton of volume and get subscription base revenue and that works for them.

758
00:57:25,840 --> 00:57:31,280
But for me, I was really like, this is like a kind of like a purist app.

759
00:57:31,840 --> 00:57:32,720
It's not going to be for everyone.

760
00:57:33,040 --> 00:57:36,480
It's kind of a narrow use case and I cannot support like...

761
00:57:36,640 --> 00:57:42,560
like the people who are just going to download it to use it as a list app or make it or want it to go in a different direction that I don't want it to go.

762
00:57:43,360 --> 00:57:47,680
And so the thinking was like, if people bought the app, like they're invested in it already.

763
00:57:47,920 --> 00:57:49,440
Because it's hard to get someone to buy an app.

764
00:57:49,920 --> 00:57:50,800
I'm sure you know this too.

765
00:57:51,200 --> 00:57:57,920
More than ever, people are not going to plunk down money unless they feel really drawn to what the app is and the problem it's going to solve for them.

766
00:57:58,320 --> 00:58:06,000
Because, you know, once you get past the people that are just super nice on Twitter and bought your app just to support you or to check it out, you get to the real customers.

767
00:58:06,160 --> 00:58:09,280
And, you know, and that's when it's like, this is a real product that they paid for.

768
00:58:09,360 --> 00:58:11,920
Even though it's like your pet project, like they paid money for it.

769
00:58:12,000 --> 00:58:12,880
So you want to take care of them.

770
00:58:13,520 --> 00:58:21,760
And that's something I really learned a lot at Buffer is we have such a high emphasis on treating customers well and having like best in class support.

771
00:58:22,000 --> 00:58:29,280
It kind of influenced my decision making on the monetization because they were already invested in SpinStack if they paid money for it.

